>Consider that your god sometimes restores your ability. Does that mean that
>after getting a message like: "You feel clumsy..." you should pray and expect
>restoration? The logic is that your god will aid you when you require divine
>restitution but he will not be your celestial nurse and pamper you.

>Thus, my solution would be:
>1) put yourself in a position where the God can legitimately come to your 
>aid. (Eat a rotten corpse.) Pray, and hope that he (or she) will benevolently
>uncurse your objects.
>2) carry around a blessed scroll of removed curse in a sack.

Completely wrong. You probably feel that it is right, but almost
everything is incorrect.

First, you don't need to be in distress to be allowed to pray. ONLY
your timeout determines this. When your timeout is not back to zero
yet, you cannot pray succesfully whatsoever (with two exceptions).

IF your timeout is at zero, you may pray, and when you pray, your god
MIGHT solve one of your worst problems, see above for examples. He will
only cure ONE problem for you, so you really shouldn't put yourself in
a position where "the God can legitimately come to your aid", since
then, you are SURE that he will not uncurse your stuff, since he has
already solved another of your problems.

To restore your abilities, you should (a)pply an uncursed or blessed
unicorn horn, so your god can do something else for you. Also note that
when you have NO problems, and you pray (assuming your timeout is at
zero), NICE things can happen to you, instead of remedies for
not-so-nice things. You might gain an artifact, a blessed spellbook, or
your MAX HP might be increased by five. These things won't happen when
you only pray when you are in trouble. Also, if you pray often enough,
you will more often see the message "You feel wise!", since praying
exercises your wisdom.

